pancake is a *codec-neutral* CapTP core: the session reasons in messages, and a
codec turns those messages into bytes and back (the
[Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) page sets up that seam). Two ship today.
[OCapN](https://github.com/ocapn/ocapn) settles the wire format as
[Syrup](https://github.com/ocapn/syrup), and pancake speaks Syrup wherever the
protocol calls for it. [Cap'n Proto](https://capnproto.org/) is the second codec, a
fixed-width, word-aligned alternative to Syrup's variable-length byte records.

[One message, two spellings. Syrup lays it out as variable-length byte records and Cap'n Proto as fixed 8-byte words; decode either and you get back the same message.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-codec.svg'))

## What a codec is, and is not

A codec only reads and writes bytes; it never decides what a message *means* or
whether it is *allowed*. That authority stays in the session, so changing codecs
changes the wire and never the behavior. Decode the same message through either
codec and you get the same borrowed view; encode through either and the bytes come
back equivalent. A build check holds that line: the deliver/resolve and
bootstrap-fetch scenarios produce identical observable traces on both codecs.

## The two that ship

| Codec | Wire shape | Reach for it when |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Syrup | length-prefixed, variable-length byte records | anything on the far side might not be pancake (it is the default, and the only codec that reaches Goblins or any other OCapN peer), or per-connection density matters |
| Cap'n Proto | fixed 8-byte-aligned words, every field at a known offset | two pancake peers, after measuring that fixed-offset access pays for the larger frames |

### Syrup

Syrup is the interop floor: CapTP 1.0 over Syrup is what pancake speaks by default
and the only thing it can speak to a
[Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/) peer or any other
implementation. Its records are length-prefixed and variable-length, which keeps
frames compact and lets the decoder double as the stream framer: one parser finds a
frame's boundary and decodes it in the same pass.

Syrup also carries a second job no other codec takes over: it is the canonical form
pancake signs. A session's `op:start-session` location signature and every handoff
certificate are Ed25519 over canonical Syrup bytes, even when the conversation
itself is riding Cap'n Proto.

Reading a Syrup value means walking its records in order: a byte record has no fixed
offset, so jumping to element *i* of a list re-scans from the start. That is the
price of compactness. The engine's steady-state path iterates forward on purpose, a
single pass over a borrowed view, so it never pays that price.

### Cap'n Proto

Cap'n Proto is the second codec: a layout of 8-byte words where every field sits at a known
offset, reached by following struct and list pointers. pancake's is hand-rolled against the
wire format with no schema compiler in the build, because it is a *pancake-to-pancake dialect*
with no third-party reader to satisfy; a generated schema would buy nothing. Both peers
settle on it at the handshake (`pairedWithCodec` in Zig, `pancake_peer_new_negotiated` over
the C ABI, the matching idiom in every binding), and the whole conversation rides it from
there. It never reaches a Goblins peer.

Fixed offsets are the point. Reaching field *i* is arithmetic, not a scan, so random access
is constant-time and the borrowed decode projection is cheap. That is the edge the
benchmark's faster Cap'n Proto decode reflects. The cost is size: padding every value to a
word and spending words on pointers makes frames larger, most visibly for the small messages
that dominate control traffic. Cap'n Proto can also carry a typed value (an abort reason, for
example) where Syrup projects a string.

## The tradeoffs, plainly

Neither codec is "the fast one." They trade along four axes, and only the first is a hard
constraint.

- **Interop is decisive.** Syrup reaches every OCapN peer; Cap'n Proto reaches only another
  pancake that negotiated it. If anything on the far side is not pancake, the choice is made
  for you.
- **Wire size favors Syrup.** Variable-length records pack a small message into far fewer
  bytes than word-aligned padding plus pointers. At pancake's high-fan-out target, smaller
  frames mean less bandwidth and less in-flight memory per connection. The gap narrows once
  a payload's own bytes come to dominate the padding, as with a large blob or a
  long string.
- **Access pattern favors Cap'n Proto.** Fixed offsets give constant-time random access
  where Syrup must scan its records in order. The engine's forward iteration means the
  steady-state path does not lean on the difference, but a workload that seeks within
  large, field-heavy messages is where Cap'n Proto earns its frames back.
- **Signing is always Syrup.** Whichever codec carries the call, the handshake and handoff
  signatures are over canonical Syrup. Choosing Cap'n Proto changes the traffic encoding, not
  the security substrate.

The same four axes, at a glance:

| Axis | Favors | In a phrase |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Interop | Syrup | the only codec a non-pancake peer speaks |
| Wire size | Syrup | no word padding, no pointer words |
| Random access | Cap'n Proto | field offsets are arithmetic, not a scan |
| Signing | always Syrup | signatures are over canonical Syrup whichever codec carries the traffic |

Two things are *not* trades. Both codecs decode view-first, a borrowed projection over the
frame with owned materialization only when the session retains a message, so zero-copy is
not a reason to pick one. And both answer to the same model: a value the model does not
admit is rejected with a typed error by either, never silently coerced.

## What the benchmark does and does not say

`zig build bench-codec` times one representative call, a method invocation with a small
mixed-type argument list, and the [Performance]($link.page('performance')) page shows the
result. On that message Syrup encodes a little faster and into a much smaller frame, and
Cap'n Proto decodes a little faster; the MB/s figures look lopsided only because Cap'n Proto
is moving several times as many bytes for the same message. Read per message, the two are
close.

That closeness is the headline for *typical* CapTP traffic, which is small control
messages. It is not a verdict that Cap'n Proto is pointless: its advantage is
constant-time field access over large, structured, or partially read payloads, a
different message shape than the benchmark measures.

## Which to reach for

Stay on **Syrup** unless you have a measured reason not to. It is the default, the interop
floor, and the signing substrate, and for ordinary traffic it is as quick and far more
compact. Reach for **Cap'n Proto** only on a pancake-to-pancake link whose payloads are large
or field-heavy enough that constant-time access pays for the bigger frames. Confirm it
with `zig build bench-codec` on the message shapes you actually send rather than this page's.

## Bring your own

The set is open on purpose: the codec layer is a registry, not a fixed switch. A
Zig consumer declares `pub const pancake_codecs` in the root source file, binds a
wire id to an implementation, and the same core speaks it with no change to the
session above. The registry resolves at compile time, so a third codec costs the
dispatch nothing the two built-ins did not. Bringing one is a Zig-consumer move:
the C ABI and the four bindings negotiate among the codecs the archives were
compiled with, which today means Syrup and Cap'n Proto. The
[architecture doc](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/architecture.md)
spells out the codec layer's contract.
